  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nToday highs will be in the 80s to near 90. Temperatures will warm \nback up into the 90s tomorrow and Wednesday as the upper ridging \nbuilds back eastward with 850 mb temperatures of 20-24C. Moisture \ntransport will increase into Wednesday ahead of a cold front \nbringing soupy dewpoints (70-75F) into the central part of the \nstate. There is a 50-80% chance of heat indices greater than 95 \ndegrees south of I-70 Wednesday afternoon, from the LREF.\n\nThe cold front will move through on Wednesday as the ridge gets \nbroken down by an upper low moving over Canada. It will clear out \nthe sweltering mid to upper 90 temperatures, leaving highs in the \nlow to mid 80s before building back to upper 80s to low 90s for the \nweekend.\n\nThis front will bring the next chance for rain Wednesday into \nThursday. CAMs are now within reach of Wednesday morning. The \nNAMNest and the RRFS are showing a line of showers & convection \nalong the front entering the northwest around 12z Wednesday. The \nRRFS suggests the initial line of convection will dry out during the \nday. Then some redevelopment in the southeast in the evening, \npotentially training near and south of I-70. PWATs in that area are \nforecast to be 2.0-2.5 inches. WPC has a marginal risk ERO south of \nI-72 and SPC has added a marginal risk for severe weather south\nof a Jacksonville to Hoopeston line for Wednesday. Damaging \nwinds are the most likely hazard with the line of storms. \n\nAdditional low-end chances exist for showers and thunderstorms \nthrough the weekend.",
